The Rust crates under `crates/` and the TypeScript admin under `web/` are a reimplementation rather
than a copy of upstream's PHP application. They keep upstream's data model and API compatible: the
`o:` JSON-LD prefix, the `/api` surface, the member names and the database schema are upstream's, so
a client written for Omeka S and a database written by Omeka S keep working. The license, the
provenance and this statement are the whole of the attribution requirement, and the upstream project
is credited in the license text and in `README.md` as well.

The default vocabularies in `crates/omeka-cli/data/default-vocabularies.json` are extracted, at the
fork point, from the RDF files of the four vocabularies upstream installs:

  Dublin Core (dcterms)          http://purl.org/dc/terms/
  Dublin Core Type (dctype)      http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/
  Bibliographic Ontology (bibo)  http://purl.org/ontology/bibo/
  Friend of a Friend (foaf)      http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/

Each vocabulary keeps the license of its own project. The extracted file holds the terms, their
labels and their comments, and nothing else.

